LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Money in another costume", "12", "<p>Beyond the Sales Console, the sidebar carries a row of consoles that look like services — reservations, staff perks, phone credit, customer lookups, stock moves. This module's thesis strips the costumes: <b>every extended counter is money in another costume</b> — each console a financial instrument wearing a service's clothes, and each governed accordingly. The track's sixth law, previewed.</p><p><b>The instruments named.</b> The walk-through that reframes the module: <b>LAY-BY</b> is a customer deposit held against reserved stock — the firm holding the customer's money (a liability) AND the customer's goods (stock committed but not sold): two exposures in one friendly arrangement, which is why its deposit percentage, its schedule and its termination all got profile-level governance in module 2. <b>STAFF PURCHASE</b> is employee credit — the firm lending against future wages, with a limit, an exposure, and a payroll recovery: the Payroll track's variable-pay machinery wearing a shopping basket. <b>AIRTIME</b> is prepaid stock whose value IS the code — inventory that is spent the moment it is revealed, the closest thing the counter sells to cash itself. The <b>CUSTOMER DESK</b> is the receivable's face — balances, invoices and payments read per person. And <b>INVENTORY OPS</b> is custody in motion — stock asked for, sent, and received under signatures. Five costumes, one wearer.</p><p><b>Why the reframe governs.</b> The consequence drawn: the trade runs these counters casually precisely BECAUSE they look like services — the lay-by book nobody reconciles, the staff purchases 'sorted at month end', the airtime float that is 'about right', the phone-number customer file with four spellings of one woman — and every casualness is a money control skipped because the money wore a costume. The certified counter reverses the instinct: each console gets the governance its INSTRUMENT deserves — the lay-by aged like the receivable it is, the staff limit enforced like the credit line it is, the airtime slip guarded like the cash it is — and the module teaches each console twice over: the mechanics (what the screens do) and the instrument (what the money is doing underneath).</p><p><b>The module's map.</b> Lay-By created (Chapter 2) and managed to its end (3), Staff Purchase with its payroll mesh (4), Airtime and the consumed-on-issue law (5), Sales History and the Customer Desk with the merge machinery (6), Inventory Ops and Label Printing with the Message Hub as their channel (7), Okelewo's extended quarter (8), and the patterns and the law (9). One discipline carries from the door: every console in this module produces documents into the same posted world the Sales Console does — the extended counters are not side ledgers; they are the one ledger, extended — and module 8's reconciliations will read them all.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Lay-By: the reservation created", "13", "<p>The customer wants the goods and hasn't the money today; the shop wants the sale and cannot give the goods away. Lay-by is the governed middle: goods reserved against a deposit, the balance paid over time. The creation chapter.</p><p><b>Creating a lay-by.</b> The four steps: the items the customer is reserving searched and added (the cart's craft from module 4, unchanged — batches and serials included, because reserved goods are still tracked goods); the customer identified by <b>phone number</b> (the lay-by is never anonymous — a reservation needs a reachable party, and the phone is the identity the follow-up call will use); the total reviewed with the required <b>deposit computed from the profile's deposit percentage</b> (module 2's number doing its work — Okelewo's fifteen percent, priced against the abandoned-basket risk); and the deposit TAKEN — upon which the <b>instalment schedule is created from the profile's instalment count and day spacing</b> (the 0-30-60 arithmetic, materialised as dated obligations).</p><p><b>The schedule as the instrument.</b> The created lay-by read as what it is: each instalment carries its <b>due date, amount due, amount paid, balance and status — unpaid, partly paid, paid or overdue</b> — which is a receivables ledger in miniature, per customer, per reservation. The instrument's two sides restated from Chapter 1 and made concrete: the DEPOSIT is the customer's money held (the firm's liability until the sale completes or the lay-by terminates — module 8's reconciliation will expect the lay-by deposits to be countable, because they are), and the RESERVED GOODS are stock committed (out of the sellable pool in practice, still the firm's in law — the reservation that is never tracked is how the trade sells one popular item twice).</p><p><b>The creation's disciplines.</b> The craft around the mechanics: the deposit is TAKEN, never promised (the lay-by without its deposit is a free reservation — the profile computes the minimum, and the counter collects it before the goods are set aside), the schedule is EXPLAINED at creation (the customer leaves knowing the dates and amounts — the printed schedule doing the explaining, so the week-forty phone call is a reminder, not a surprise), the reserved goods are physically SEGREGATED where the shop's layout allows (the lay-by shelf — custody made visible, per the Inventory track's instincts: the reserved kettle on the sales floor is an accident waiting for a busy Saturday), and the phone number is VERIFIED at entry (rung once, or read back — the reservation reachable being the whole design's precondition). The chapter's closing line: the lay-by created properly is a clean instrument — money held, goods held, obligations dated, party reachable — and everything Chapter 3 manages flows from this creation being done right.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Lay-By: managed to its end", "13", "<p>Every lay-by ends one of two ways — completed or terminated — and the console manages both, plus the long middle. The management chapter.</p><p><b>The Open Lay-Bys panel.</b> The working list: everything outstanding, each with its schedule visible — and the panel read as the aging report it is: the instalment statuses (unpaid, partly paid, paid, <b>overdue</b>) are the read, and the OVERDUE line is the action — the follow-up call made from the recorded phone number, on a rhythm (module 8's calendar gives the lay-by review a weekly line), because the lay-by left to age silently converges on the trade's classic mess: goods held for a ghost, a deposit nobody can match to a person, and a shelf of inventory in limbo.</p><p><b>Make Payment.</b> The instalment collected: the amount entered, the payment method chosen — <b>the schedule updates, instalments moving to paid or partly paid</b> — and the payment landing on its method for the close's per-method count (module 4's tender-truth law: the lay-by instalment is money like any other, counted at the shift close like any other — and the manual's own note that <b>lay-by activity is summarised in the shift close</b> makes the ritual's window include this console explicitly). The collection craft: payments accepted in any pattern the schedule tolerates (the customer paying two instalments at once, or half of one — partly paid exists for a reason), the printed receipt updating the customer's picture each time, and the final instalment's completion RELEASING the goods — the reservation becoming a sale, the deposit and instalments becoming revenue, the shelf clearing.</p><p><b>Terminate.</b> The other ending, governed hard because it is money moving backwards: <b>Terminate cancels the lay-by</b> — the refund method chosen, and <b>a manager's email and password required</b> (the termination is an authorised event by construction — module 5's anatomy: actor, authoriser, trail — because the terminate-and-pocket is the lay-by's native fraud shape, and the manager gate is its wall), upon which <b>goods return to stock</b> (the reservation released, the shelf restored, the inventory truthful again). The termination's policy layer, for the consultant: the refund's ROUTE follows the returns posture (store credit where cash refund is off — the deposit coming back as stored value keeps the money in the ledger), the deposit-forfeiture question is decided as POLICY before the first dispute (the profile's deposit is a minimum, not a penalty schedule — the firm that intends to keep abandoned deposits writes it on the printed schedule and says it at creation, or it keeps arguments instead), and the terminated lay-by's record survives (the instrument's history readable — who reserved, who paid what, who authorised the end). The chapter's closing frame: managed properly, the lay-by book at any moment is three honest numbers — deposits held, balances due, goods reserved — each reconcilable, which is all module 8 will ask of it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Staff Purchase: the employee credit line", "13", "<p>The staff member buys today and repays from wages — a benefit the trade runs on trust and this counter runs on a limit. The employee-credit chapter.</p><p><b>The purchase flow.</b> The four steps with the instrument visible in each: the <b>employee identifier</b> entered — and the screen showing the credit picture whole: <b>purchase limit, current exposure, remaining balance, and estimated monthly repayment</b> (the employee sees their own position before adding a single item — the transparency that makes the limit a fact rather than a surprise); the items added with the <b>staff discount applied automatically to every line</b> (module 2's percentage — profile precedence over company-wide — executing without counter judgment: the staff price is policy, not favour); the total reviewed against the remaining balance with the hard rule: <b>a purchase that would exceed the limit is REFUSED</b> (the credit fence as physics — no override path by design, because the limit's owner is the employee record's owner, and the exception belongs there, not at the till); and completion <b>booking the purchase to the employee purchase account and scheduling it for payroll recovery</b>.</p><p><b>The Payroll mesh.</b> The wiring restated from module 2 and read as the control chain it is: the company-wide settings name the <b>employee purchase account</b> (where exposures sit until repaid — a receivable from staff, visible to finance as a balance, reconcilable per person) and the <b>default salary component</b> (the payroll deduction that recovers it — the Payroll track's variable-input law receiving the counter's feed), while the <b>purchase limit lives on the employee record</b> (set by the employment relationship's owner, not by the shop floor). The mesh's discipline: the counter ORIGINATES the exposure, payroll RECOVERS it, finance READS the account — three functions, three records, one instrument — and the certified consultant verifies the whole chain at enablement (a test purchase, through the account, into a payroll cycle, recovered) because the half-wired version (purchases booked, recovery never configured) is an interest-free loan book growing quietly under a benefits banner.</p><p><b>The panel and the direct repayment.</b> The management layer: <b>Open Staff Purchases</b> lists outstanding balances — searchable by staff name or reference, each showing total and balance — the staff receivable's aging view, read on the same weekly rhythm as the lay-by book; and <b>Take Payment</b> records a <b>direct repayment outside payroll</b> (the staff member clearing a balance in cash before a payroll run — the money on its method, the exposure reduced, the payroll recovery adjusting to the remainder). The chapter's closing frame: staff purchase done right is a benefit with a balance sheet — the limit set by the right owner, the exposure visible to everyone including its holder, the recovery automatic, the direct repayment possible — and done wrong it is the trade's oldest quiet leak: goods leaving through the family door, sorted at month end, until month end stops sorting it. The counter's version has no such door; it has a ledger.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Airtime: the code that is money", "13", "<p>The counter's purest instrument: airtime — recharge vouchers whose entire value is a string of digits, spent the moment it is seen. The consumed-on-issue chapter.</p><p><b>The three-step sale.</b> The console's flow: <b>Select Carrier</b> (the network chosen), <b>Choose Amount</b> (a denomination picked, a quantity set — with <b>availability shown and the sale capped at what is in stock</b>: airtime is inventory, counted like inventory, and the console refuses to sell codes it does not hold), and <b>Complete Payment</b> (the method chosen, the sale completed). Then the paper that IS the product: <b>each voucher prints on its own slip with its recharge code clearly set out</b> — one slip per code, handed over like the money it is.</p><p><b>The consumed-on-issue law.</b> The manual's warning, elevated to the module's sharpest engraving: <b>an airtime code is consumed the moment it is issued</b> — so <b>print before the customer leaves</b>, and if the printer fails, <b>reprint from the invoice number immediately rather than reselling</b>. The law's logic taught so the discipline survives pressure: the moment of issue REVEALED the code from stock — that unit is spent whether or not the paper made it into a hand — so the failed print answered by a fresh sale creates two consumed codes against one payment: one in the customer's hand, one leaked into the void (or into a pocket — module 8's map notes what a 'failed print, sold again' habit is worth). The reprint-by-invoice path exists precisely for this: <b>vouchers can be reprinted by looking up the invoice number</b> — the same code recovered, the same sale, no second consumption — and the certified cashier's printer-failure reflex is invoice number first, reseller never.</p><p><b>The instrument's stock.</b> Airtime as inventory, governed accordingly: the codes arrive as PREPAID stock (money already spent to acquire them — the float's cousin, sitting in denominations), the availability display is the stock count (the sale capped at holdings — and the reorder read coming from exactly this screen plus Revenue Intelligence's detailed sales: Okelewo's own dashboard shows recharge vouchers as the flagship's TOP sellers, which makes airtime stockouts a revenue event, not a shrug), and the reconciliation is arithmetic module 8 will formalise: codes received minus codes issued equals codes held — a count as countable as the drawer, run on the same rhythms. The chapter's closing line: the airtime counter compresses this track's whole philosophy into its smallest form — stock that is money, a slip that is the product, a law that survives printer jams, and a count that either reconciles or tells a story.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Sales History & the Customer Desk: the record, per person", "13", "<p>Two lookup consoles that turn the counter's past into answers: the transaction record, and the customer's whole picture. The record chapter.</p><p><b>Sales History.</b> The counter's own register: recent transactions FILTERED by date range, SEARCHED by reference or customer name, NARROWED to voucher activity, a single cashier, or a single shift — with <b>returns marked by a Return label</b> so they are never mistaken for sales — and any entry OPENED to its lines and recorded payments, reprintable from there. The console's daily use case, taught as the manual gives it: <b>the customer with a lost receipt</b> — searched by name, or by any fragment of the reference — served in a minute from the record (module 4's two-reference law completing here: the offline local reference finds the sale as surely as the posted one). And the supervision preview: the cashier and shift filters are module 8's investigation entrance — the specific person, the specific period, read from the same console the floor uses.</p><p><b>The Customer Desk.</b> The receivable's face, in three views: <b>DETAILS</b> — contact information, price list, credit limit, payment terms and loyalty points, alongside the figures that make the desk an instrument reader: <b>outstanding balance, total invoiced, total paid, total orders, average order value, purchase frequency, last purchase and return rate</b> (the customer read as a relationship AND a risk in one screen — the return rate line being the desk's quiet fraud sensor: the customer whose return rate is a third of purchases is a pattern module 8 reads by name); <b>INVOICES</b> — everything bought, filterable by status and date, searchable by number, printable; <b>PAYMENTS</b> — receipts with reference, date and amount. Plus <b>Edit Customer</b> for the name, mobile, email and loyalty card — the master maintained where it is used.</p><p><b>The merge machinery.</b> Module 2's duplicate defences, completed with the repair tools: the <b>automatic merge on creation</b> (on by default — a new customer merging into an existing one when the MOBILE NUMBER matches: dedup at birth, left on unless numbers are genuinely shared), and the <b>merge tool</b> for the ones that slipped through — scanning for customers sharing a mobile or email, the surviving record CHOSEN, history merged into it — with the property that makes it safe to use: <b>the merge is REVERSIBLE</b> — a snapshot kept, cancellation restoring both records. The rhythm and the reason, from the manual: run the duplicate scan <b>monthly</b>, because <b>loyalty depends on it</b> — a customer split across two records has their points split too, and will complain — and the desk's whole value (the balances, the rates, the history) presumes one person, one record: the master hygiene law, at the counter's file. The chapter's closing frame: these two consoles are where the counter's evidence layer pays out daily — the lost receipt found, the dispute settled from the record, the customer read whole — and the certified professional treats them as the first stop for every 'the customer says' conversation, because the record was built precisely so nobody has to remember.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Inventory Ops, labels & the Message Hub: custody in motion", "13", "<p>The counter's stock consoles: what is held, what is asked for, what is moving — plus the labels that price the shelf and the hub that carries the traffic. The custody chapter.</p><p><b>The Inventory tab.</b> Stock as the terminal knows it: filtered by WAREHOUSE (where the profile's other-warehouses permission allows — the 'do you have it at the other shop?' answered from the chair), by item group, and by free search over code, name or brand; the COUNTERS summarising items in stock, low on stock and out of stock (the shelf's health in three numbers); and the <b>synchronising marker</b> shown while figures refresh — the local-copy honesty visible (the number on screen is the copy's number; the marker says when it is being trued).</p><p><b>Material Requests.</b> Stock asked for, as a document: <b>New request</b> — the requesting warehouse confirmed, the SOURCE warehouse chosen, a <b>priority</b> set (low, medium, high or urgent — the queue's triage vocabulary), each item and quantity added, and a NOTE telling the supplying branch what is needed and why, then submitted. Both directions visible: <b>requests you have raised and requests raised against you</b> both appear, with a detail panel carrying the full item list and status — the inter-branch ask as a governed conversation (documented, prioritised, answerable) instead of the trade's version: a WhatsApp voice note and a hope.</p><p><b>Stock Transfers.</b> Goods actually moving, under signatures: a transfer RAISED, and — the receiving discipline the manual states and this academy underlines — a transfer RECEIVED by <b>confirming quantities as you accept them, so a short delivery is RECORDED rather than absorbed</b>. The one clause carries the Inventory track's whole custody law: the receiver who confirms what actually arrived (eleven of twelve) creates the discrepancy record at the boundary where it is cheap; the receiver who accepts the paper quantity absorbs the short into their own branch's shrink, where it surfaces months later as an unattributable count variance — the confirm-quantities habit is the difference, and it costs ten seconds per line.</p><p><b>Label Printing and the Hub.</b> The two supporting consoles: <b>Label Printing</b> — items added singly by search, or IN BULK (an entire item group, or the lines pulled from a goods receipt or stock entry — the new delivery labelled without retyping), per-row label COUNTS, the template chosen (module 3's proofed presets), preview, print — and <b>available even offline</b> (the shelf priced through the outage). The <b>Message Hub</b> — notices, transfer requests and alerts filed like a mailbox (unread, starred, transfers, requests, system, alerts, archived; searchable; important notices pinned) — with the operational teeth: <b>transfer and request messages carry the underlying detail and can be ACTED ON without leaving the hub</b>, and the unread count rides the top-bar envelope. The hub read as the channel discipline of two tracks: the counter's operational traffic in ONE governed stream — acknowledged, actioned, archived — rather than scattered across personal phones. The chapter's closing line: custody in motion is still custody — the request documented, the transfer confirmed at the boundary, the shelf labelled from the receipt, the traffic in the channel — and the counter that runs these four consoles as designed has extended the warehouse's laws to its own back corner.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: Okelewo's extended quarter", "16", "<p>The extended counters go live in their second quarter — demand-led, instrument-governed. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>Lay-by, launched and tested (Ch. 2-3).</b> Customer demand brings lay-by first (the season's big-ticket items — the reservation questions arriving daily at the flagship). The creation discipline holds from day one: deposits computed at the profile's fifteen percent and TAKEN, schedules printed and explained, the lay-by shelf physically established behind the counter, phone numbers read back at entry. The quarter's test arrives on schedule: an overdue lay-by ages through two missed instalments — the weekly panel read flags it, two recorded calls go unanswered, and the TERMINATION runs as designed: manager's credentials at the gate, the refund routed to store credit per the returns posture, the goods returned to stock the same hour, the record surviving whole. The MD's note on the forfeiture question — decided as policy BEFORE this case, printed on the schedule since launch — means the retained deposit portion generates zero argument: <i>the schedule said what the schedule said.</i></p><p><b>Staff purchase, wired whole (Ch. 4).</b> The flagship's benefit launches only after the consultant's full-chain test: one real purchase through the employee purchase account, into a payroll cycle, recovered by the salary component — proven end to end before the second employee touches it. The quarter's numbers stay boring by design: limits visible to their holders, one exceed-limit refusal (absorbed without argument — the screen showed the arithmetic), one direct repayment through Take Payment ahead of a payroll run, and finance reading the account balance per person at month end. The old 'family door' — goods sorted at month end from memory — closes without an announcement; the ledger simply replaced it.</p><p><b>Airtime, the surprise flagship (Ch. 5).</b> The dashboard's truth from module 1's screenshots becomes the quarter's operational focus: recharge vouchers are the flagship's TOP sellers — six hundred units of one denomination in a month — which promotes airtime stock to a managed float: availability read daily, reorders triggered from the detailed sales report, and the consumed-on-issue law drilled until reflexive. The drill pays in week nine: a printer jam mid-sale, and the cashier's reflex runs the designed path — invoice number looked up, slip REPRINTED, the same code recovered — where the untrained answer (sell another) would have leaked a unit into the void. The received-issued-held count joins the close ritual's rhythm and reconciles all quarter.</p><p><b>The record consoles at work (Ch. 6-7).</b> The quarter's daily wins, logged: a lost-receipt customer served in ninety seconds from Sales History (name search, offline-era local reference found, reprint — the two-reference law in the wild); the Customer Desk's return-rate line flags one account at thirty-one percent — read, investigated, and resolved as genuine (a size-run issue with one product line: a buying insight wearing a fraud sensor's clothes — the reading mattered either way); the first MONTHLY merge scan finds nine duplicate pairs (four auto-merge misses with typo'd numbers, five legacy), merged with the survivor chosen and one merge REVERSED next day when a shared family number surfaces — the snapshot doing exactly what the design promised; and the receiving discipline records its first short delivery — eleven of twelve confirmed at the flagship against a Distribution Centre transfer, the discrepancy documented at the boundary and resolved to a warehouse pick error within the week, where the old counter would have absorbed it into December's mystery shrink. The MD's quarter note: <i>the side counters turned out to be the main counters wearing disguises. We govern them the same now, and the month-end 'sorting out' meetings have nothing left to sort.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the sixth law", "13", "<p>The costumes' scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the service illusion.</b> Symptom: lay-bys in a notebook, staff purchases 'sorted at month end', airtime 'about right'; money controls skipped because the money wore a costume. Disease: the instrument unrecognised (Ch. 1). Fix: the reframe — every extended counter named for the instrument it is, and governed as that instrument.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the free reservation.</b> Symptom: goods set aside on a promise; deposits promised, not taken; the popular item sold twice; a shelf of inventory held for ghosts. Disease: lay-by without its creation disciplines (Ch. 2). Fix: deposit taken at the profile's percentage, schedule printed and explained, goods segregated, phone verified — and the panel's overdue line worked weekly.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the terminate-and-pocket door.</b> Symptom: lay-bys cancelled casually; deposits refunded to hands, not records; the reservation's end nobody authorised. Disease: the termination ungated (Ch. 3). Fix: the manager gate used as built — credentials, refund route per policy, goods to stock, record surviving — and the forfeiture policy written before the first dispute.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the half-wired benefit.</b> Symptom: staff purchases booked, recovery never configured; the interest-free loan book growing under a benefits banner. Disease: the payroll mesh assumed instead of proven (Ch. 4). Fix: the full-chain test at enablement — purchase, account, payroll cycle, recovery — before the second employee buys.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the resold code.</b> Symptom: printer fails, sale rung again; two consumed codes against one payment; airtime shrink nobody can explain. Disease: the consumed-on-issue law unknown (Ch. 5). Fix: print before the customer leaves; on failure, reprint from the invoice number, never resell — and the received-issued-held count on the close rhythm.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the absorbed short.</b> Symptom: transfers accepted at paper quantities; shorts surfacing months later as unattributable count variances; branch shrink carrying the supply chain's sins. Disease: receiving without confirmation (Ch. 7). Fix: quantities confirmed as accepted — the short recorded at the boundary where it is cheap — plus the request documented, the traffic in the hub, the duplicates merged monthly.</p><p><b>The sixth law.</b> The costumes, retired: <b>every extended counter is money in another costume — the lay-by a deposit against reserved goods, created whole and ended under authority; the staff purchase a credit line proven through payroll; the airtime code consumed on issue and recovered by invoice, never resold; the customer one record, merged reversibly; and custody in motion confirmed at every boundary.</b> Module 7 opens the programme built entirely of stored value: the vouchers — from mint to breakage.</p>"),
]

